WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:06.009
Sound effects make movies movies… but there’s
a bit of sausage making going on here: sometimes

00:00:06.009 --> 00:00:08.600
you’re better off not knowing where those
sounds originated.

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:12.560
So start banging your coconut shells together
as we gallop through the secrets behind some

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:15.560
of the most bizarre sound effects in recent
movie history.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:21.240
Star Wars sound designer Ben Burtt is viewed
as a legend in movie production circles for

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.269
his sound effect creation methods — and
with four Oscars to his name on beloved productions

00:00:25.269 --> 00:00:28.660
such as E.T. and the Indiana Jones series,
it's no surprise.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:33.510
The next time you or one of your friends swings
an invisible lightsaber in an imaginary battle,

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.440
just remember who came up with that unique
swoosh noise.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.180
"Not as clumsy or random as a blaster.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:45.020
An elegant weapon, from a more civilized age."

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:50.480
Working on the 1977 space fantasy classic,
Burtt found an old film projector and blended

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:53.940
its sound with an old TV to create the distinctive
lightsaber noise.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:57.051
"When they just sat there and idled it made
a wonderful humming sound, which would just

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.350
slowly change in pitch."

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:03.840
However, even a maestro like Burtt needed
a touch of luck, and the whirring element

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.680
of the lightsaber's sound was discovered completely
by accident.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:10.692
He managed to capture the iconic sound by
thrusting a shotgun microphone back and forth

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:11.960
in front of a speaker.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:18.080
The inventive sound designer for James Cameron's
$102 million masterpiece Terminator 2: Judgement

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:23.160
Day came up with some frugal and unusual methods
to make the film to sound as good as it looked.

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:28.040
The special effects for the film were spectacularly
groundbreaking and ate up a large portion

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:29.400
of the then-record budget.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:33.780
This makes Gary Rydstrom's achievements with
the sound effects budget all the more impressive.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:38.860
Amazingly, an everyday item played a key role
in completing one of the film's coolest scenes.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:43.640
Robert Patrick's T-1000 is chasing John and
Sarah Connor through a mental asylum, but

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:45.840
a row of jail bars appear to block him.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.160
But he's liquid metal, so he just goes right
through.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:02.360
Rydstrom revealed that he came up with a very
cost-effective way to create the sound: he

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:06.540
turned a dog food can upside down and slowly
eased the congealed food out.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:11.410
The quality of Rydstrom's methods was matched
only by his creativity, which yielded another

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:14.610
unexpected and comical money-saver for Terminator
2.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:19.200
By submerging a microphone wrapped in a condom
into a thick flour-water mix and then blowing

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.760
Dust Off into it, he was able to create the
perfect sound for the T-1000's shape-shifting

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:24.260
abilities.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:35.400
Beneath the mines of Moria lives the terrifying
fire demon known as the Balrog, which shows

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.460
up just in time to ruin the day for Frodo
and his friends in The Fellowship of the Ring.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.420
The sound of that blood-chilling roar was
achieved by dragging a concrete block slowly

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:55.400
across a wooden floor while horses and donkeys
were layered in to fill out the sound.

00:02:55.750 --> 00:03:00.740
It was the work of sound designer David Farmer,
who also created the sound of Moria orcs after

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:02.420
a visit to a marine mammal center.

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:03.620
He told Empire:

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:07.540
"It was the time of year they had lots of
baby elephant seals there.

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:11.550
[...] So we recorded ourselves scrambling
around wearing cleats for the movement, but

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:14.660
the major signature sound for them was the
elephant seal pups."

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:27.400
Dinosaurs have been extinct for thousands
of years, so obviously, no one really knows

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:28.280
what they sounded like.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:31.020
No, not even the people who worked on Jurassic
Park.

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:40.300
Although the finished product sounds fitting
in Jurassic Park, it does make you wonder

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:44.820
how wrong film makers have gotten when it
comes to movies featuring prehistoric creatures.

00:03:45.069 --> 00:03:50.620
In a 1995 documentary, director Steven Spielberg
told sound designer Gary Rydstrom to, quote,

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:54.020
"go make dinosaur sounds that didn't sound
like Godzilla or Rodan."

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:58.250
What's really fun to me sometimes is just
having something on a keyboard and performing

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:02.500
it giving it a try seeing how it works."

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:07.360
Rydstrom's approach was to find a creature
that scientists estimate existed close to

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.220
the time of dinosaurs: tortoises.

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:13.580
The shelled creatures did provide the right
sound bite, but how it came about was both

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:19.220
strange and unforeseen: Rydstrom sat around
for hours recording tortoises while they mated.

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:29.699
Does anyone know what a giant rolling boulder
sounds like as it bears down on you after

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:32.120
a booby trap in an ancient temple has been
triggered?

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:43.400
No, but Raiders of the Lost Ark sound designer
Ben Burtt managed to create a pretty convincing

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:46.810
approximation by using an old Honda Civic
on a gravel road.

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:52.020
"And we just coasted down this road, and as
the car accelerated in the sense of gathering

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:55.980
speed that ended up really being the basis
for the giant boulder"

00:04:56.270 --> 00:05:00.300
To record the sound, Burtt had to hang out
the back window of the car with his boom mic

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.880
pointing down toward the tires.

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:04.940
Does that make him the Indiana Jones of sound
design?

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:06.020
Eh, maybe!

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:10.860
The webs that shoot from his wrists have saved
Spider-Man on many occasions, but how do you

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:13.160
decide on a sound for such an unusual tool?

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:21.180
Spider-Man director Sam Raimi had high standards
when it came to what he wanted, and sound

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:24.600
department veteran Charles Maynes did his
best to come up with something unique.

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:28.990
Initially there were failed experiments with
blow darts and chemical rockets before the

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:31.540
crack of a bullwhip got the whole process
started.

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:36.980
With the first layer down, the second came
from a very unexpected place: a feral cat

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:40.520
that Maynes himself rescued from the pound
who would attack him every time he tried to

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:41.020
feed her.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:47.920
"And she would spit, and that spitting sound
was one of the principle components for the

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:49.640
actual shot."

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:54.140
It's honestly amazing that putting an angry
cat in the sound design worked out so well.

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:56.440
That decision could have gone horribly wrong.

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:01.100
"Oh No… no"

